    "created_at": "2023-09-27T15:18:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-09-27T15:18:58Z",
    "body": "> I don't think this is specific to popovers. What about dialogs? What happens when you disconnect a video or audio element from the document?\r\n\r\nWhen you remove a `<video>` or `<audio>` element from the DOM, the HTML spec has the UA pause. Search for [\"When a media element is removed from a Document, the user agent must run the following steps:\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/media.html#media-element).\r\n\r\nIn https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9793, it's been proposed to add a `\"autopause\"` attribute to media elements (and perhaps a similar property to `<iframe>`s to handle the potentially more common case of embedded media player. But I would flip that argument; the desirable behavior would be to _always_ pause playback of media elements inside a `<dialog>` or `popover` when they are dismissed. \r\n\r\nThe behavior specified for \"when you remove a media element from the DOM\" is there because removing a playing `<video>` or `<audio>` element also removes the user's ability to pause playback. That is also true for dismissed `<dialog>` or `popover` elements, and that those elements remain in the DOM is merely a implementation detail, and not a user expectation.\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
